  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nUpper impulse that brought light rain to the area early this morning \nis now tracking across eastern MO. Meanwhile, a northern stream\nwave is lifting across the northern Rockies. At the surface, \ncold front extends from central IA to just south of the KS/NE\nborder and started to wash out a couple hours ago.\n\nA broad area of southwest flow aloft will setup across the Plains \ntonight and will persist through Wed night. Still a small chance for \na few showers and storms late tonight southeast of the KS Turnpike \nas low level jet develops increasing moisture transport. Widespread \nhighs around 80 are likely for Wed with these readings around 15 \ndegrees above normal highs for this time of year. Fast moving upper \nimpulse will slide across the Northern Plains on Wed and will push a \ncold front into north/central KS Wed evening. A few showers/storms \nmay develop along the front Wed night with the better chances over \nnortheast KS. \n\nFocus will then shift to a southern stream impulse set to lift \nacross sw/central TX on Thu night and into OK by Fri morning. \nThis will overspread showers across much of KS Fri and \ncontinuing through at least Sat morning. There are some model \ndifferences that will need to be worked out that will have an \nimpact on rainfall location and timing. The ECMWF is further \neast and much faster with the upper impulse compared to most of \nthe other models. Currently have a lean to the slower and \nfurther west solutions which may delay on the onset of rainfall \non Fri and linger a bit longer on Sat. PW values through this \nperiod look to be 175-200% of normal, so some decent rainfall \namounts will be possible. However, the fairly progressive nature\nof this wave should keep flooding in check. \n\nMedium range models are still digging a more significant trough \nacross the Great Basin/Desert Southwest starting Sun evening. We \nshould see the initial area of mid level warm advection precip Sun \nnight into Mon morning. While the timing is different, both \nECMWF and GFS close-off this system as a deep upper low over \nwestern OK/TX Panhandle in the Mon night-Tue time frame. This\nwould place much of the forecast area in the deformation zone\nof this system which would result in widespread cold rain and \nstrong winds.",
